          <li>
            <Tile client={s} lazy />
            <Caption client={s} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec tight" id="add-ons" aria-labelledby="addons-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <h2 id="addons-h">Add to any project</h2>
        <p>Photos when needed, and brand work when you want it on its own.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pair pair-wide">
        <div class="card panel keyed addon" id="photography">
          <span class="pip" aria-hidden="true">
            P
          </span>
          <div class="line">
            <h3>Photography</h3>
            <p class="amt">{photography.price}</p>
          </div>
          <p>{photography.note}</p>
          <p>
            When the photographer and the designer are one person, the photos match the layout
            instead of fighting it. Images arrive edited, web-optimized, and ready to drop in.
          </p>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li>On-site Sunday or weekday coverage</li>
            <li>Headshots for team and staff</li>
            <li>Environmental photography of your space</li>
            <li>Edited, web-optimized images delivered with the website</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="t-go">
            <a class="cta" href="/contact/?service=photography">
              Ask about photography
            </a>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card panel keyed addon">
          <span class="pip" aria-hidden="true">
            B
          </span>
          <div class="line">
            <h3>Brand &amp; strategy</h3>
            <p class="amt">{brandStrategy.price}</p>
          </div>
          <p>{brandStrategy.note}</p>
          <p>
            If you already have a logo, palette, and type system, I use it. If you do not, brand
            work can be folded into the strategy phase or scoped separately.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec tight" id="care" aria-labelledby="care-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <h2 id="care-h">Care plans</h2>
        <p>Year one hosting is included in every build.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="intro">
        A website is not something you print once. Hosting, security, domains, and certificates keep
        moving, so every site I launch is quoted with care. After year one, Hosting is the part
        every live site needs, and the monthly levels add edit time for when you would rather not do
        it yourself.
      </p>
      <p class="intro">
        Every site I look after sits on one status board. It checks every 15 minutes that the site
        is up, and tracks how fast it answers, whether deploys and backups ran, known security
        alerts in its software, and domain renewals where I manage the domain. If a site goes down,
        my phone tells me, so I usually know before you do.
      </p>
      <ul class="care-cards">
        {carePlans.map((c) => (
          <li class="card keyed care-card">
            <h3>{c.name}</h3>
            <p class="amt">
              {c.price}
              <small>{c.period}</small>
            </p>
            <p>{c.who}</p>
            <p class="care-time">{c.time}</p>
            <ul class="ticks">
              {c.features.map((f) => (
                <li>{f}</li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <div class="card tbox">
        <table class="care">
          <caption class="vh">
            The four care plans compared: price, who each is for, edit time, what's included and
            reply times
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <td class="corner"></td>
              {carePlans.map((c) => (
                <th scope="col">
                  <span class="pl">{c.name}</span>
                  <span class="amt">
                    {c.price}
                    <small>{c.period}</small>
                  </span>
                </th>
              ))}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Who it's for</th>
              {carePlans.map((c) => (
                <td>{c.who}</td>
              ))}
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Edit time</th>
              {carePlans.map((c) => (
                <td>{c.time}</td>
              ))}
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Replies</th>
              {replies.map((r) =>
                r ? (
                  <td>{r}</td>
                ) : (
                  <td class="n">
                    <span class="vh">Not included</span>
                  </td>
                ),
              )}
            </tr>
            {careRows.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{row.name}</th>
                {row.has.map((y) => (
                  <td class={y ? 'y' : 'n'}>
                    <span class="vh">{y ? 'Included' : 'Not included'}</span>
                  </td>
                ))}
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="after">
        Unused hours do not roll over. Anything beyond your plan is quoted in writing first. Sites
        with custom tools need {care.name} or above. Change or cancel with 30 days notice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec tight" data-mfold id="how" aria-labelledby="how-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <h2 id="how-h">How it works</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Start with a conversation</h3>
          <p>
            The first conversation is free: a call about what you actually need, and an honest
            ballpark before anyone is committed. If you go ahead, the project opens with a working
            session, an hour together in person or on video, that ends with a one-page brief you
            sign off on.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Then the strategy</h3>
          <p>
            I write down who the site is for, how the pages fit together, and what you want visitors
            to do next. You read it and push back, and I revise. The strategy doc is the source of
            truth for every design choice that follows.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Then I design and build</h3>
          <p>
            Components, content, and pages built in the open. Preview links land in your inbox at
            every milestone so you can react early instead of seeing the whole thing for the first
            time at launch.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Launch, and follow-through</h3>
          <p>
            I launch the site with you, then stay close for the first month to fix anything that
            surfaces. After that you have documentation and a simple way to reach me when something
            comes up.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <details class="fold steps-fold" id="timelines">
        <summary>
          <h2>Timelines</h2>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="fold-a">
          <div class="fold-in">
            <p class="intro">The brief at the end of strategy sets the real timeline.</p>
            <div class="card panel span">
              <div class="bar-row">
                <p class="bar-t">
                  <b>Most websites</b>
                  <span>Six to ten weeks from kickoff to launch</span>
                </p>
                <div class="bar" aria-hidden="true">
                  <i style="--a: 6; --b: 10"></i>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="bar-row">
                <p class="bar-t">
                  <b>Larger projects with custom photography or multiple content collections</b>
                  <span>Twelve to sixteen weeks</span>
                </p>
                <div class="bar" aria-hidden="true">
                  <i style="--a: 12; --b: 16"></i>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p class="bar-scale" aria-hidden="true">
                <span>Kickoff</span>
                <span>16 weeks</span>
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sec tight folds">
    <div class="wrap rows">
      <details class="fold" id="every-build">
        <summary>
          <h2>In every build</h2>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="fold-a">
          <div class="fold-in">
            <div class="pair">
              <div class="card panel">
                <h3>The site itself</h3>
                <p>
                  Modern, accessible, fast websites, built on Sanity or WordPress, whichever suits
                  the people running yours.
                </p>
                <p>
                  I match the build to your budget and to whoever will maintain it after launch.
                  Everything is built mobile-first and tested for accessibility from the very first
                  screen, then handed over with documentation.
                </p>
                <ul class="ticks">
                  <li>A custom design built to your brand, not a stock template</li>
                  <li>
                    Fast pages, with fonts and images served from your own site on a custom build
                  </li>
                  <li>Layouts that work on every phone, tablet and desktop</li>
                  <li>Accessible from the first screen, checked against WCAG AA</li>
                  <li>Hosting set up for you, with year one included</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="card panel">
                <h3>Strategy, first</h3>
                <p>
                  Strategy always comes first, because the design has to serve a plan. It starts
                  with a working session together, then I write a short brief that the rest of the
                  project follows.
                </p>
                <ul class="ticks">
                  <li>A working session, then back-and-forth until the plan is right</li>
                  <li>Who the site is for, and what you want them to do next</li>
                  <li>Content inventory of what you already have</li>
                  <li>A one-page brief the rest of the project follows</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                  Strategy is the front of every project, not a line item. The prices above already
                  include the session and the brief, and year one hosting too.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="fold" id="why">
        <summary>
          <h2>Why one person</h2>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="fold-a">
          <div class="fold-in">
            <p class="intro">
              Plenty of people will quote you less, and for some sites that is the right call. Here
              is what this price buys.
            </p>
            <ul class="whys">
              <li>
                <h3>One person, no handoffs</h3>
                <p>
                  The person who plans your site designs it, builds it, and, when needed, shoots the
                  photos. No agency markup, and nothing lost in translation between a strategist, a
                  designer, and a developer who never quite talk to each other.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <h3>Built to be handed off</h3>
                <p>
                  Every site is set up so the next volunteer or staff member can keep it running. If
                  you ever move on, the code, content and documentation go with you.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <h3>Accessible by default</h3>
                <p>
                  Every custom build is made to WCAG AA and checked until each page scores 100 for
                  accessibility in Lighthouse. For a church, school, or nonprofit it is the right
                  thing to do, and it is included, not an upsell.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <h3>Priced where you can see it</h3>
                <p>
                  The tiers, the care plans and the five-year sums are all on this page, so you can
                  check them against anything else you are looking at before you ever talk to me.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="tools">
              I build with modern tools, including AI-assisted workflows, the same way I use
              frameworks and a camera. They help one person do the work of a team. The strategy, the
              design, and the accountability stay mine, start to finish.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

